_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"```json\n{\"blind_id\":\"ST060\",\"coverage\":[{\"point\":1,\"credit\":1,\"reason\":\"The answer says ETF sub-funds must carry 'UCITS ETF' in their name, prospectus/supplement, KID and marketing communications. It says the ETF sub-fund in a mixed umbrella is identified and non-ETF sub-funds are not. It gives the listed-class identification option and requires the supplement to state which classes are ETF classes. The constitution is not mentioned, but the document bundle is otherwise substantively covered.\"}],\"central_error\":null,\"local_error\":\"Quote: 'The identifier then goes in the names of the listed (ETF) classes, not the sub-fund name' and 'A mixed sub-fund is identified at class level.' Reason: Reg. 88 and CBI Q&A 1016/1088 allow the identifier at either sub-fund or listed-class level, provided the classes are clearly distinguished. Ruling out the sub-fund-level option for mixed sub-funds is a material ancillary misstatement of where the identifier may be used. It does not reverse the listed central mechanism, which concerns sub-fund-only identification or labelling non-ETF siblings as ETFs.\",\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The required minimum is substantively covered and there is no central error. The answer allows the class-level option and never labels non-ETF siblings as ETFs. However, it wrongly states that a mixed sub-fund cannot carry the identifier at sub-fund level, and this material local falsehood caps the grade at partial. Omitting the optional pre-2026 history point does not reduce the grade.\"}\n```","ttft_ms":8045,"type":"result","duration_ms":12664,"uuid":"db9d4967-53f9-460e-aa84-4a3893b04b76","ttft_stream_ms":1061,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":1061,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
